@Tutorial(time: 15) {
    @Intro(title: "Using CucumberSwift with Carthage") {
        Add CucumberSwift to a unit test target in an Xcode project with Carthage, write a first feature file, and let CucumberSwift suggest its step definitions.

        You end up with the setup of the [GettingStarted](https://github.com/cucumberswift/CucumberSwiftSample/tree/main/Tuist/GettingStarted) sample, in an iOS app's unit test target: a `Features` folder, and step definitions that use Cucumber Expressions.
    }

    @Section(title: "Add CucumberSwift to your test target") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            You need Xcode 16 or later, Carthage 0.37 or later, and an Xcode project with an iOS app and a unit test target. If your project has no unit test target yet, choose File → New → Target… and add a Unit Testing Bundle. Choose XCTest as its testing system.

            This tutorial differs from the GettingStarted sample in its platform only: the sample is a macOS unit test target that gets CucumberSwift with Swift Package Manager. The feature file and the step definitions are the same.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Add CucumberSwift to your Cartfile, then run `carthage update --use-xcframeworks --platform iOS`. That builds `CucumberSwift.xcframework` in `Carthage/Build`.

                @Code(name: "Cartfile", file: "Cartfile")
            }

            @Step {
                Select your unit test target and open its Build Phases. Expand Link Binary With Libraries and select the add button (+). Choose Add Other… → Add Files…, and select `Carthage/Build/CucumberSwift.xcframework`.

                @Image(source: "Carthage-Link-Framework.png", alt: "The Link Binary With Libraries phase of the unit test target, with CucumberSwift.xcframework added.")
            }

            @Step {
                The test bundle must contain the framework too. Select the add button (+) at the top of Build Phases and choose New Copy Files Phase. Set its destination to Frameworks, and add `CucumberSwift.xcframework` to it.

                @Image(source: "Carthage-Copy-Files.png", alt: "A Copy Files phase of the unit test target, with the destination Frameworks and CucumberSwift.xcframework in it.")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Add a Features folder") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            CucumberSwift looks for your `.feature` files in a folder named `Features`, inside the test bundle. The name is case sensitive. Xcode must copy the folder into the bundle as a folder, so add it as a folder reference.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                In the Finder, create a folder named `Features` in your project's folder, next to the folder of your test target.

                > Important: Don't create it inside your test target's folder if that folder is a synchronized folder (the default for targets created in Xcode 16 and later). Xcode copies each file of a synchronized folder into the bundle on its own, without the `Features` folder, and CucumberSwift then finds no features.

                @Image(source: "CreateFeaturesFolder.png", alt: "The Finder, showing a new Features folder next to the test target's folder.")
            }

            @Step {
                Drag the `Features` folder into the project navigator. In the options sheet, reference the folder in place rather than copying it, add it as a folder reference, and add it to your test target only.

                The folder appears blue in the project navigator. Xcode copies it, with everything in it, into the test bundle.

                @Image(source: "AddFolderReference.png", alt: "Xcode's options sheet for adding the Features folder as a folder reference to the unit test target.")
            }

            @Step {
                Add a feature file to the `Features` folder. You can group feature files in subfolders.

                @Code(name: "Calculator.feature", file: "Calculator.feature")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Write the step definitions") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            CucumberSwift finds your step definitions through an extension of `Cucumber` that conforms to `StepImplementation`. When a step has no definition, CucumberSwift writes one for you.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Replace the test file Xcode created in your test target with this one. `bundle` returns the bundle that holds the `Features` folder: your test bundle.

                The extension adds a conformance to a type and a protocol that both come from CucumberSwift. `@retroactive` says you mean it. Without it, Xcode warns about the conformance, in Swift 5 and Swift 6 language modes alike.

                @Code(name: "StepDefinitions.swift", file: "StepDefinitions-Empty.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Run the tests with ⌘U. They fail, because no step definition matches the steps in `Calculator.feature`. Each failure points at its step's line in the feature file, and its message contains a step definition for that step.

                > Note: A step that has the same definition as an earlier one, such as `And I have entered 3 into the calculator`, gets no message of its own.

                @Image(source: "UndefinedStepFailure.png", alt: "The Issue navigator, showing the failure for an undefined step at its line in Calculator.feature, with the suggested step definition in the message.")
            }

            @Step {
                CucumberSwift also attaches every suggested step definition to one file. To open it, right-click the test `GenerateStepsStubsIfNecessary` in the Test navigator and choose Jump to Report. Under Pending Steps, open the file whose name starts with `GENERATED_Unimplemented_Step_Definitions`. Xcode adds a unique ID to the end of the file name.

                @Image(source: "ViewGeneratedStubs.png", alt: "The test report for GenerateStepsStubsIfNecessary, with the GENERATED_Unimplemented_Step_Definitions file under Pending Steps.")
            }

            @Step {
                Paste the suggested definitions into `setupSteps()`. Each one matches its step with a regular expression literal and fails until you replace its `XCTFail` line with your test code.

                > Note: Regular expression literals need iOS 16, tvOS 16 or macOS 13. For an older deployment target, write the steps as Cucumber Expressions, as in the next step.

                @Code(name: "StepDefinitions.swift", file: "StepDefinitions-Generated.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Replace each `XCTFail` line with your test code. You can keep the regular expressions, or rewrite them as Cucumber Expressions, as here: `{int}` matches a whole number, and `match.first(\.int)` reads it back as an `Int`. Run the tests again. They pass.

                See <doc:Matching-Steps> for every way to match a step.

                > Note: Leave parallel testing off for your CucumberSwift test target: the "Execute in parallel" option on that target in your scheme or test plan. XCTest's parallel workers do not run CucumberSwift's scenarios correctly. Depending on the setup, each scenario runs once in every worker, or not at all, and the test run can still pass.

                @Code(name: "StepDefinitions.swift", file: "StepDefinitions.swift")
            }

            Your test target now has the setup of the [GettingStarted](https://github.com/cucumberswift/CucumberSwiftSample/tree/main/Tuist/GettingStarted) sample in [CucumberSwiftSample](https://github.com/cucumberswift/CucumberSwiftSample), a working, tested project that you can compare with yours. Its next sample, TestNavigator, shows how your scenarios read in Xcode's test navigator. See also <doc:Running-Tests-In-Xcode>.

            CucumberSwift also runs in a UI test target. The setup is the same: add CucumberSwift, the `Features` folder and the step definitions to the UI test target, and drive your app from the steps with `XCUIApplication`. To test UIKit screens from a unit test target instead, see <doc:CucumberSwift+UIUTest>.
        }
    }
}
